{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792436616,"nonce":"+xzIT/8uEhRQDXAjF30pJARQg1A=","message":"Tokens: a syntax token was an anonymous object of 193 bytes, and a 120 KB file has 28,760 of them, 5.5 MB; kept as three integers each (12 bytes) they are 0.35 MB. Landed in this commit.\n\nA caution on measuring it: `hl.Gc.stats().currentMemory` and the collector's live figure count blocks that hold any live line, so a freed token inside a block with other live objects frees nothing in them. Building a coloured document allocates about 32 MB of garbage between the objects it keeps (twenty of them showed 358 MB of footprint against 100 MB for plain ones); allocating afterwards reused about two thirds of that, so it is mostly fragmentation, not retention.\n\nFollow-up: the Haxe highlighter makes a string for most comparisons (`line.substr(i, 2) == \"//\"`), which is the garbage; comparing character codes would cut the 32 MB. The bracket map costs about 365 bytes an entry (6,374 entries, 2.3 MB in the same file); a sorted pair of integer arrays would be a tenth of that.","files":null}]}